[{:seon.task/name "tests-fork-from-the-branchs-warm-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests2. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Tests: the runtime rail forks from the branch's warm agent; selection is a set operation; results are branch facts. Deletes: bin/test, bin/prove, bin/land (gone), test_runner, the file cache, the results branch, shared roots. DESIGN (tests2 with flow, 2026-11-16): a test child is forked by the system process (the one fork point), never by the branch's agent: an agent is multithreaded (Flow fibers, effect workers), fork() copies one thread and inherits the others' held locks, and today seon.agent/fork-test-child is refused (:seon.test/fork-refused) whenever another thread is active. The child, forked at main's program, runs load-diff! of its branch's changed forms (O(d), ms a form), then its tests on world values (d/with): no disk, no checkout, never collecting what it inherited. Cost: a fork ~1-5 ms, d forms, ~21 ms a test. The branch's agent only asks (seon_prove); the fork op is runtime's. OPEN, measured: (1) one judge plan allocates 167-372 MB and keeps the branch graph, base graph and branch changes live in the agent, all private under fork-first: the plan's state becomes structural sharing over the system process's held graph, the agent holding diffs only; (2) each agent's Datahike node cache fills privately (deps); (3) the fixtures' live size in the system process is unmeasured. RULER: the 5 MB rows become a platform test on the candidate binary (fork, read the footprint, collect, read, one read, read, a merge, read), so each READY quotes one repeatable result; the footprint is seon.lib/real-mb (phys_footprint, deps), one reading an eval. THE RULER, interface agreed with runtime and deps (2026-11-16): seon.fork-server-test/a-forked-agent-holds-its-memory-rows, a platform test on the one booted candidate (seon.test-store/candidate answers {:port :system-pid :writer-pid :branch b}, built and booted once a test run; deps), reached through seon.server/ask on :port (:attach, :eval, :prove). It attaches an agent on its own branch b (seon.system/spawn! in the writer process; runtime), reads seon.lib/real-mb in one eval each after the fork, after (System/gc) and after one read of 100 datoms (the read its own eval before the gauge), then makes a program move on b (an eval defining a form) and takes the same three readings from a fresh agent on b; the system process's own by seon.system/fork-server-stats (runtime) before and after. Asserts each agent row <= :seon.config.agent/own-mb (5), and the system process after the move <= its row before plus the moved forms; answers every reading as data on the result, so each READY quotes one run key. Until the rail exists, the same steps run by hand as writer-process code on the standing system. MEASURED by hand, deps' binary, 2026-11-16: the GAUGE is a fork's PRIVATE dirty pages (vmmap's SM=PRV, the kernel's private count by pid: a native call, deps), never phys_footprint, which charges a fork for copy-on-write pages still shared with its parent (runtime: of a fresh agent's 206 MB dirty heap, 165 MB SM=COW, 40.7 MB SM=PRV; tests2, agent pid 63513 after its first eval and two plans: of 370 MB dirty heap, 327 MB COW, 43.7 MB PRV, plus ~21 MB malloc). So a working agent's own memory is ~65 MB against 5 MB, about 13x; the footprint rows first quoted (137, +232, +75 MB) overstate it and are withdrawn. The ruler reads each row from outside, by pid, on the private gauge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-red-in-the-daily-full-test-is-a-task"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: yes, and \"how do we make sure we don't keep creating new tasks?\" What exists: the daily bin/test --all on main answers seon.prove/full-answered, which raises one :platform problem, :seon.prove/full-run-red, naming every red deftest, and that problem never closes as the reds are fixed one by one. Correct: each red deftest is one held task whose identity is the deftest's name (a task named from the var, as the failing deftest seon.agent-test/foo gives one task entity), written by upsert, so the next day's red on the same deftest is the same entity, never a new one; a green of that deftest in a later full test closes it, and the one problem goes. No hash: the var's name already identifies it. Proofs, red first: two full answers with the same red deftest leave one task; a green answer after it closes that task; two different reds make two. See docs/sharing.md#a-result-stands-until-what-it-read-changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/two-full-answers-name-same-red-deftest-leave}} {:seon.task/name "the-daily-full-run-builds-its-own-libraries"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-09 (audit of the 2026-11-08 landings): the daily bin/test --all on main (runs/merge-tests/<commit>-<uuid>) has been red three days running on seon.db-test/stored-values-compress-through-the-codecs-the-build-made, its first assertion: konserve.jolt.zstd/lib-path is the main tree's target/lib/libzstd.dylib, not under the run's own checkout. The run's checkout takes the main tree's libraries (bin/env's JOLT_ZSTD_LIB and JOLT_LZ4_LIB, from the environment it was started in) where bin/build would make its own. The test is right (a store's codecs are the ones the build made); the daily run's environment is wrong. Find where the run's checkout is made and started (seon.fx's :tests effect, the main-build slot) and make its bin/env name its own target/lib, as a worktree's does. Proof: the test green in the next daily run; no test changed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/stored-values-compress-through-the-codecs-the-build-made}}
 {:seon.task/name "main-past-its-last-full-run-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (2026-11-10): bin/test --all writes full-run.edn beside the verdict cache (the commit, :green or :red with each red, when), and bin/full-run says how many landings main is past it (bin/land before LANDED, bin/system status); the dashboard says nothing. The record stays a file, so a store erase keeps it (docs/merging.md#the-full-run-is-the-net). Plan: root's bridge (seon.system/landed-tx, bin/land's) writes on root's entity, with each landing, the landings since the last full run, its commit and its outcome, read from the file by bin/land and passed as data (root reads no file); a problem class shows it past :seon.config.proofs/full-run-landings (a new key: how many landings main may move past the net; provenance to be stated, the owner's to set) and when the last outcome is :red, naming the reds. No clock: a count of landings. Proof, red first: a landing past N with the last full run green is one row; a red last run is one row naming its reds; a landing within N with a green run is none."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/landing-count-past-full-run-landings-key-green}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-service-runs-each-item-once-for-every-asker"
  :seon.task/plan "The test system as a Flow (docs/merging.md#the-test-service; owner, 2026-11-10/11: \"I want the flow system that is always only letting the tests run that we have cores for\"). Built and on main by 2026-11-11: one queue of (test, digest) items for every asker (seon.prove/ask-items), each run once; admission by root's :process effect owner, by the processes each class holds (seon.fx/admits?); an agent's turn first; process tests, check steps, builds and seeds as items on a run's checkout; every asker hears every red it waits on (a turn its proofs, a tree's run every item). Left, the one piece no other task holds: a seon.cost proof that dispatch stays microseconds an item (grant scans the queue: O(queued) a grant; at 2,000 queued items that is the cost to bound). The pieces after it are their own tasks: seon.prove/the-shell-runner-is-deleted, seon.fx/process-tests-run-from-inside, seon.check/check-steps-are-tests-on-the-service, seon.prove/the-daily-full-run-is-a-service-run. Proof to write: seon.prove-cost-test/a-grant-costs-what-it-scans. Design approved by the owner 2026-11-15 (notes tests-4-design-one-queue-2026-11-15.md): this is landing 13. One queue: seon.prove's proc owns the items (a test, or a folded step, at its digest), dedups askers by digest, orders by critical path (a task's own proofs first), its in-port bounded so askers park. One admitter and its workers: the :process owner (system.edn's :process, {:perform seon.fx/run-item}) admits by weight (a test child 1 process at its class's measured peak bytes; a system test 3, ~0.5-1 GB) and hands an item to a free worker, which forks the warm child from a branch agent at a turn boundary. Every prover is a client: bin/test, bin/prove, (my.agent/prove), a round's agent, later seon_done; one pend per asker's run, answered in seon.prove's own step at its last verdict; ends: the answer, an item's typed :error (a worker died: only its item), end-all at a stop, the asker's EOF. No clock. Deletes: seon.proofs/shards, shard-N.edn and test-runner's --shard mode, bin/stalls, the fold's :shards, serve-item! and the agent's :tests proc, :seon.config.proofs/jobs and every session load check; bin/land's prove becomes a client of this queue as soon as warm forks run the branch's program (runtime's landing 5), not only when seon_done carries the lands: then admission covers lands and the herds end; until then the local path is bin/land's alone. Estimate [H]: a typical change (R ~216 reached) plan + R x 21 ms / cores + one fork ~ 1-3 s of test work; new code the clients and the item perform; net src negative. Needs: runtime's admission (landing 11) and its one loader (landing 5)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/an-item-two-askers-want-runs-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-digest-whichever-runner-plans-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-11: one test on one program, seon.prove-test/a-worker-pulls-one-item-at-a-time, is digest 22c228440ccf3874 by the shell and 33dfdedd17e20795 by an agent of the standing system: the agent planned by empty inputs ({:tree \"\" :env \"\" :config {}}, seon.agent/test-inputs' fallback). Root hands agents the binary's inputs only when three tree ids agree (seon.system/booted-source: the binary's tree, bin/tree's with untracked files; the source fact's :tree, the catch-up's; target/seon.inputs' :tree), and a stray untracked file in the main checkout at build time (src/demo/math.clj, a write-back that reached the owner's working copy) made them differ, so every agent digested by nothing: no green shared with the shell (batch 82 through the system: 370 verdicts in 29 minutes of 1,600 items), and digests that omit pins and config. Design, ruled 2026-11-11 (the managing session's go; the owner may overrule): inputs stop depending on a tree id. A boot computes the inputs of the files its catch-up read (bin/system start, which can run git, hands them to root as it hands the port: seon.fx/inputs over the checkout as read), and root's :seon.system/source holds them; seon.agent/test-inputs reads them, and with none answers the typed :seon.agent/inputs-unknown, never an empty default (that lands with this, never before). Deleted: booted-source's tree equality, target/seon.inputs' tree keying, the test runner's write of it. Then plain bin/prove asks the system again (piece c), once a landing through it ends within a shell prove's time. Proofs to write: one tree planned by the shell and by an agent gives every test one digest; an agent whose root holds no inputs refuses to plan. One source (tests-4, 2026-11-15; owner: \"the runner is supposed to use the store\", \"the DB IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH\"). Measured at root [M]: a plan of main's checkout found 0 of 2,104 tests green at their digest, and the runner's digests and the store's agreed on 78 of 2,104 on one commit. Causes found: (1) the store's import read the importing process's live vars (fixed 767888acc9, macros interim 12877b06b9; a re-import of the 88 stale contracts is computed, awaiting root's write); (2) seon.agent/test-plan digests the branch with inputs from the branch db (branch-inputs) but the base with root's boot snapshot (seon.agent/test-inputs), so once main moves past root's boot, :seon.env/schemas (~1,150 tests) and 4 config keys look changed for every test: on one branch it selected 2,063 tests against 739 by reach, where fresh graphs of branch and base differed in 5 rows. Fix, in order: (a) base and branch inputs both derived from their own db (schema entries, config entities, :file/text), never the boot (extends seon.agent/branch-inputs); (b) test-plan's :selected becomes the fast lane's one test list, measured at root at 3.2 s first, 1.1 s again; (c) the worktree runner reads the program from the store and bin/test is a thin wrapper over the system; deletes test-runner/tests-of's file import (program-db, the per-tree program stores, reach-graph-*.fasl and reads-index-*.fasl), so no second program exists to agree with; (d) an agent's reads index takes [:file p] digests from its db, not git blobs (today none: agent.clj reads-index with {} blobs, so an agent reruns every green keyed by a file). Also measured: importing one test file into an agent costs 128M ticks and 525 MB (13 s wall at load 45), ~10x what its forms should cost; and the import hook fires only on Edit/Write, never on a Bash edit. Step 2 of the inputs, agreed (tests-4, runtime-2, data-2, root; 2026-11-15), done in E: every input a test plan digests is derived from its own db, never from a boot snapshot: schema entries (seon.program/schema-changes, data-2: the one function), config entities, :file/text and :seon.pin/*, with the binary's identity from deps' seon.fx/runtime-for digest. Deletes seon.system/booted-source's :inputs and my.root/landed!'s rewrite of them. Why [read, runtime-2]: my.root/landed! refreshes main's :seon.system/source inputs only on a no-restart landing; a merge through seon_done or the writer refreshes nothing, so the snapshot drifts between landings (the class of values fixed at boot that go stale under in-place loads). Step 1 (landed with seon.agent-test/a-branch-that-changes-no-schema-selects-no-test-for-its-schemas): the base is digested through the same seon.agent/branch-inputs as the branch. Config source: not seon.config/from-db (a stored copy data-2 is deleting or making the one source); fx/config-input over the held config until data-2's plan names the one source."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-test-has-one-digest-whichever-runner-plans-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-no-asker-waits-on-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (batch 82): the managing session ended its landing's client, and the standing system went on running the tree's 1,370 queued items with no one waiting, until a restart. The service has no cancel, and a tree's :judge run waits on every item as its proof (every red to every asker), so an ended agent does not end it either; process and step items run under root's effect owner. Plan: an asker's connection that ends (the server's ask-streamed peer gone) withdraws it as a waiter (seon.prove/answer-asker's removal, which already drops a queued item no one waits on); a run with no waiter left ends, its running items' processes ended by their group (f2, bin/bounded); and a cancel by run key for a person. Proof to write: a run whose asker disconnects leaves no item queued."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-run-no-asker-waits-on-is-ended}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proves-systems-end-with-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (Web; Data model earlier): readable's whole prove of 20:58 left 7 processes alive 3 h 16 m, three `seon root --profile scripted` and their bin/held drains (--detach --drain, one reparented to init), all under that tree's own target/runs/shard-* directory. A process test's root is started detached on purpose (it must outlive the test's request), so nothing ends it when the shard, or the prove, ends; bin/end-tree walks by parent and misses a detached group (the same class as the shard orphan Data model captured at 20:55). Plan: a shard's run directory is the key: at a shard's end, and again at the prove's end, every process whose command line names that run directory is ended by its process group after bin/evidence, and the prove prints any pid it could not end, never silence. Under the service the same holds per item (Optimization's bin/bounded ends an item's group; f2). Proof to write: a prove whose process test leaves a root running ends it, and its last lines name none left."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/a-shards-roots-end-with-its-run}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-shell-runner-is-deleted"
  :seon.task/plan "Piece e of the test system (docs/merging.md#the-test-service; owner, 2026-11-10: \"I don't want 'shards' anymore\"). What exists, 2026-11-11: plain bin/prove and bin/prove --merge (bin/land's) ask the standing system, which indexes the checkout into a branch (seon.system/prove-tree!) and proves it under its one admission (jolt/tests-sysprove). The shell runner stays only behind bin/prove --out, --tests and --checks, bin/check with no argument, the daily full run (seon.fx full run: bin/test --all) and the item bodies (bin/test --shard, bin/check --steps). Plan: once seon.fx/process-tests-run-from-inside and seon.check/check-steps-are-tests-on-the-service leave no item body calling it and the daily run is a service run (seon.prove/the-daily-full-run-is-a-service-run), delete test_runner's plan-of path and shards (write-shards!, worth-shards, memory-jobs, :seon.config.proofs/jobs), the proved marker (bin/prove's MARK), bin/stalls (its timer is bin/bounded's), and bin/prove's shell half. Proof: seon.main-test's whole-src check that nothing names bin/test --shard, red until the last caller goes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/bin-prove-is-a-thin-client-of-prove}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-daily-full-run-is-a-service-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization (seon.fx). The daily full run (seon.prove full-request, :full mode) is a :tests effect that runs bin/test --all in a worktree of main (fx.clj, the full run's body): its own plan and shards, outside the service's admission. Plan: the full run is a request of the service like any other, its items every test and step of main at its digest with reuse off (:fresh? true items), so one admission covers it and its verdicts land as any run's do; seon.prove/full-answered's red-as-problem stays. Then the full run's shell body has no caller (seon.prove/the-shell-runner-is-deleted). Proof to write: a :full request's items are every key of main, none reused. Replacing the cron (root and flow, 2026-11-15; flow extends it): once main's by-change prove works (the test service proving main on every landing), an idle-queue re-verify replaces the 20:00 UTC cron (config/local.edn :seon.config.proofs/full-run-at). It starts when main's head has moved and the :prove proc's queue is idle; it runs every test not yet run at that head at the lowest priority, admitted so it never competes with a land; and a red names the merge that moved main to it. The cron is deleted once recorded reads are proved complete by a mutation proof. The last daily run (03:04 local, 7cf0c01b64) had 9 reds: 7 cost tests from before the ticks switch, and seon.db-test/stored-values-compress-through-the-codecs-the-build-made and seon.db-test/the-newest-n-cost-what-they-hold; tests-4 reruns them by name on main and files what is still red."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/the-full-run-is-one-request-of-the-service}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-binds-the-machine-keys-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (the whole-load trial's control): run in an agent of the standing system, five seon.prove-test tests went red because the service's init read the admission in force, which there includes the main tree's config/local.edn (max-load 18.0), so a busy machine held their items: a verdict the machine's load could turn. Fixed in seon.prove-test (proc0: the shipped workers, no load bound) on jolt/tests-sysprove. The class: a test whose verdict reads a machine key (max-load, max-workers, max-cores, ports, cores, memory) through the config in force judges the machine it runs on. Plan: sweep the tests that reach seon.config/value of a :seon.config.machine/* or :seon.config.proofs/max-* key outside a binding of their own (the reach graph's config-reads names them) and give each its own value; then a law: a deftest's reach never reads a machine key unbound. Proof to write: the sweep's count is zero on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-verdict-reads-no-machine-load}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-queue-orders-by-work-not-wall-time"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Proposed 2026-11-11 (mail 1727, code read): seon.prove's grant orders queued items by item-value, (waiters + age) / ms, where ms is a test's measured WALL time (seon.fx/typical-ms, the median of its greens' :ms) or :seon.config.proofs/unmeasured-ms. Wall time is load: the same test measures 3x under a busy machine, so the order (never a verdict) moves with what else ran. Correct: order by work, as cost proofs measure it (docs/cost.md#what-is-measured): an in-process test's ticks (its verdict holds :ticks), a process item's or step's child CPU ms (rusage, which the runner can record beside :ms), falling back to the estimate only where none is recorded. Deletes the wall-ms input of item-value. Proof: two queued items whose wall ms disagree with their ticks are granted in tick order."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/the-queue-grants-by-recorded-work}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-gate-run-is-answered-when-every-item-has-its-verdict"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen on the standing store (Optimization, 2026-11-12, read at a REPL on main): the one task that reached the merge gate, seon.ctx/a-cites-part-shows-forms-not-templates, holds 242 deferred tests and 78 :seon.task/deferred-results (75 pass, 3 fail). Its results were written at 17:03:22Z, 7 s after a red verdict (seon.source-test/a-branchs-config-is-its-own-and-reaches-the-next-process at 17:03:15Z); of the 164 tests with no result, 94 have verdicts in the cache written from 17:00:58Z to 17:29:45Z, after the gate was answered, so the run was answered while its items still ran. By design (seon.prove/item-ended, 32e94c68b1 on main since 2026-11-11 14:26Z) a :judge run waits for every item past a red: ask-items gives its asker every item as a proof. Not yet known: whether the standing binary at that boot predated that commit, or whether the gate's run (seon.system/prove-tree! from seon.fx/run-tests, mode :judge, named tests) reaches its asker by another path whose :proofs is empty (item-ended then answers at the first red). First step: a test that asks a :judge run named by three process items, one red and two slower, and checks that the answer holds all three. The reconciler's side is done (jolt/redsat): a test the run answered nothing of is recorded :none and holds the task. See docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-judge-run-past-a-red-is-answered-with-every-item}}

 {:seon.task/name "one-grant-answers-differently-in-a-shard-and-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12 (Tests, building seon.prove/batch-of): a rule that let a batch take more items while its measured ms was under :seon.config.proofs/child-min-ms granted two 5 ms items as ONE batch when called in an agent (load-file of the branch's seon.prove, with and without check-private!), and as TWO batches of one in a bin/test shard on the same tree (seon.prove-test, child-min-ms read as 100 in both). Rewritten as a plain loop, the shard still gave two. The rule was dropped from the tip, so the code is on no branch; the agent's run and the shard's are the evidence (Tests' transcript, 08:36-08:38). One function giving two answers is the finding: compiled (the shard's AOT) against interpreted (load-file), or a binding the shard holds that the agent does not. Proof, red first: the same call, ask-items with two items {:agent :a1 :ms 5} on two workers and the floor rule, answers the same batches in a bin/test shard and in an agent's eval. Owner: Tests, with Deep fixes if it is the compiler."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-grant-is-the-same-in-a-shard-and-an-agent}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-service-runs-a-plans-contract-keys"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Tests. Prerequisite of deleting the shard path (schedule item shard-delete). A checkout's plan names the generative check's contract keys (seon.agent/process-plan's :mg, from jolt/tests-mergever) but seon.prove/plan-items makes no item of them, so a prove through the service runs none, where bin/test's shards ran them (test-runner/deal-mg, seon.proofs/mg-run). Plan: an :mg item kind run by the agent's fork as tests are (seon.proofs/mg-run k digest in the child, its verdict written by seon.fx/write-verdicts! under steps/mg/), granted in shares like test items. Proof, red first: a checkout run through the service with one contract key at a new digest answers that key's verdict."
  :seon.task/kind :work
